      "review_note": "official_website is null: both articles only carry an unresolved 'Official website' / 'Clavias webbplats' link label with no URL in the extract. Neither source gives a street address, legal form details beyond the AB suffix, employee count, revenue or ownership, and neither states that Clavia was ever acquired, so status 'active' rests on the Swedish article's present tense product list dated 2026-06-26 and the English article's 2025 range summary. Instrument years: where the English article gives no year, the start year of the Swedish production ranges was used; instruments still on sale (Nord Stage 4, Grand 2, Electro 6, Electro 7, Wave 2, Organ 3, Piano 6, Drum 3P, Nord Wave 2) have no year in either source and are left null, as are the rack variants Nord Rack, Nord Rack 2, Nord Rack 2X, Nord Rack 3, Nord Electro Rack, Nord Electro Rack 2, Nord Modular G2X and Nord Micro Modular. The English article's sentence about March 2012 runs the Nord Drum and the Nord C1 together, which could be read as dating the C1 to 2012, but the same sentence says a Baroque organ emulation was added to the C1 in 2009 and the Swedish article gives the C1 a 2005 to 2009 production run, so 2005 is stored. The English list names 'Nord Electro 4D / HP' and 'Nord Electro 5D / HP' while the Swedish list names 'Nord Electro 4' and 'Nord Electro 5'; the plain names are stored. The Nord Modular family is described as software-based semi-modular, and the sources do not make the keyboard versus rack form unambiguous for the Modular, Modular G2, G2X and Micro Modular, so the plain 'synthesizer' category is used for those. Nord Electro and Nord Stage are stored as 'keyboard' because they combine organ, piano and in the Stage's case synth sections. Excluded from the instrument list as non sound generating: Nord Piano Monitor (monitor boxes) and Nord Pedal Keys 27 (MIDI organ pedalboard). The 1984 rack drum system with several EPROM sound modules is folded into the 'ddrum' entry, since the English article gives it no separate product name. Peter Jubel, who helped create the first Nord Lead and co-founded Propellerhead Software, is a person and not stored as a company relation."
